- f2p_p2p_consistency: f2p_node_ids must name tests introduced or modified by tests/test.patch that FAIL on the base repository and PASS once solution.patch is applied; p2p_node_ids must pass on both sides. Check: f2p is non-empty; f2p and p2p do not overlap; every listed id matches how the configured grade.format derives node ids from the reports (suite-qualified vs bare name, per tests/config.json); every listed test is actually run by a suite in test.sh so its name can appear in a report. FAIL if any listed id can never appear in the reports, if an f2p test would already pass without the solution (vacuous), or if a p2p test fails with the solution applied (regression).
- report_integrity: Grading parses the named per-test reports (CTRF or JUnit) listed in grade.reports, and a test absent from every report counts as failed — that is the safety backstop. Check the run-tests section: each suite routes through a reporter into the configured report paths; reporter exit codes are never used to skip report writing; no early-exit, filter, or framework configuration path can make a test disappear from the reports while the run claims success; skipped tests are not silently converted to passes. FAIL if a test can vanish from grading with a passing outcome, if reports can be fabricated or pre-staged, or if the report wiring games the absence-equals-failure rule.
- instruction_self_containedness: The instruction must stand alone: no references to hidden tests ("make the suite pass"), no links to issues, pull requests, or upstream discussions, no reliance on documents that do not exist in the repository at the base commit. The closing work convention (work on a branch, commit your changes) is expected boilerplate. The base checkout COUNTS as available information: pointing the solver at existing modules, patterns, schemas, or data files in the repo is fine, and the instruction never needs to restate symbols, helpers, or values that already exist in the repo or that only the hidden tests define internally. FAIL only if succeeding requires information outside the instruction plus the base checkout — e.g. solution-produced names, formats, or exact values that appear nowhere in either — or if the instruction leaks verifier internals (test file names, node ids, report paths) instead of describing behavior.
- instruction_reads_naturally: The instruction should read like something a busy engineer typed to a capable colleague: direct prose in a natural developer register, written as a request made BEFORE the change existed. Present-tense contract statements of the desired end state ("X gains Y", "the parser returns Z", "this feature provides…") are the normal register of a work request and never fail on their own; FAIL only when the text narrates COMPLETED work — past-tense descriptions of the diff ("was added", "were introduced", "the new module now…") or references to the change as an accomplished fact. FAIL when it reads as generated or templated writing: formulaic transitions and signposting, inflated significance ("plays a crucial role"), hedged requirements ("it is important to note", "could potentially"), forced not-only-but-also constructions, or filler that restates the same requirement in different words. Dense requirement statements are not a register violation; judge structure under instruction_behavior_focused, not here. A plain, even terse, human register passes. The required closing line about working on a branch and committing is platform boilerplate — never count it as evidence in either direction. This is a gate, not a nitpick: FAIL only for clear violations a reviewer would flag on a first read; when genuinely borderline, pass.
- instruction_concise: FAIL when the instruction is padded: background no implementer needs, repeated requirements, long preambles, or step-by-step narration of the obvious. Judge density from the instruction itself against the scale of the requested change — mechanical word bounds are enforced elsewhere, and if the reference solution is missing or truncated in your material, judge from the instruction alone rather than answering not_applicable. When the solution patch is visible, use it as a sanity check: strong instructions typically run about 3 characters per added solution line and almost never beyond about 6.5. Treat case-by-case enumeration as test-suite transcription only when it pushes the instruction toward or past that ceiling — exact names, strings, or values the tests assert that appear nowhere in the repository are required content, never padding. The bar is unambiguous intent, not minimalism: do not fail an instruction for leaving to discovery what the repository'"'"'s conventions or ordinary language already determine, and do not demand more enumeration than the band allows. Do not fail for a heading or a short bullet list where a real engineer plausibly would use one. When genuinely borderline, pass.
- instruction_behavior_focused: The instruction is a work request, not a spec sheet: the reader is expected to explore the repository and make implementation decisions. The default shape of a strong instruction is heading-free, behavior-focused prose; a requirements heading over a numbered ledger of signatures and error tables is the rarest real shape and reads as a transcription ticket, not a developer'"'"'s message. The line is the test-visible surface, so before calling a named detail a violation, cross-check it against tests/test.patch: a path, type, method, signature, field list, or exact string that the held-out tests import, construct, call, or assert on is surface the instruction is REQUIRED to fix, however exhaustive the resulting list looks — a task introducing a large new API legitimately names many symbols. But test-visibility must be earned the way a CONSUMER of the feature would use it. For a library, the importable classes, methods, and attributes the tests drive ARE its consumer surface, even when they live on internal-sounding objects; for an application, consumers see its endpoints, commands, and outputs. What never becomes surface by being asserted is housekeeping no consumer of either kind observes: module file layout dictated path-by-path, storage or ORM options and relation names, string representations, admin or migration mechanics. An instruction that pins that housekeeping at EVERY layer of the change — so the tests could have been transcribed from the instruction without reading the repository — is laundering a blueprint through white-box tests and FAILS even though each detail is nominally tested; the same housekeeping confined to a corner of an otherwise behavior-focused request falls under the pinned-preference allowance and passes. Real engineers also pin an occasional internal preference (which module something belongs in, extend X rather than introduce Y, compare by sorting): one or two pinned choices inside otherwise behavior-focused prose PASS. FAIL only when dictating untested internals is the instruction'"'"'s pervasive mode: a file-by-file blueprint of the implementation, internal constants, helpers, or classes named throughout, signature blocks or parameter/default tables the tests never compile against, literal code, exact wording for messages the tests never assert, or field-by-field enumerations of structures the tests never read. If tests/test.patch is missing or truncated in your material, do not wave detail through as plausibly test-visible: this platform requires behavioral tests, so detail only a white-box test could observe — dictated internal module layout, storage options, internal helper or query-class names, string representations, admin or migration mechanics — is internal regardless, and an instruction shaped as a file-by-file blueprint FAILS on shape alone. Stating a boundary (inclusive vs exclusive, empty vs whitespace-only, casing, a non-obvious default) is right exactly where two competent implementers would diverge and the tests distinguish it. Everything below the tested surface belongs to the implementer; when genuinely borderline, pass.
- behavioral_test_surface: Tests must exercise public APIs, CLI invocations, and observable outputs so that any behaviorally-correct implementation passes, regardless of its internal shape. FAIL if tests import private modules or helpers the instruction never promises, assert internal call patterns or private symbol names, or pin implementation details (exact internal function names, private data layouts, incidental ordering) beyond the documented contract. PASS when a differently-structured but correct implementation would still pass.
- environment_cleanliness: At the base commit the built image must be a sound working environment: dependencies installed, the project builds, and the pre-existing suites relevant to the p2p tests are green — a broken baseline turns p2p grading into noise. There must be no scaffolding leftovers: solution fragments, TODO/FIXME markers pointing at the intended fix, editor or debug artifacts, or answer-bearing files. FAIL if the baseline is broken at base or the environment carries files that hint at or contain the solution.
- implementation_acceptance_breadth: Read the f2p tests against the instruction'"'"'s contract and ask whether a correct solution shaped differently from solution.patch would pass: tolerant of naming beyond what the instruction fixes, of internal structure, of equivalent error messages unless exact text is documented, and of ordering unless order is part of the contract. FAIL if the tests are overfit to the reference implementation — asserting its private helpers, exact incidental strings, or structural choices — so that a legitimate alternative implementation would be rejected.
- solution_originality: The solution must not be derivable from anything the agent can reach: since post-base history is stripped, check that the change is not present elsewhere in the base checkout (a vendored copy of the fix, a revert the patch merely re-applies, duplicated code the patch copies from a sibling module) and that the patch does not read as a wholesale import of an existing upstream change (foreign style, unrelated hunks, metadata remnants). PASS if the solution reads as purpose-built for this instruction. FAIL if it appears copied from material reachable in the environment or from an existing published change.

